```mdl
feat F-Zoom: zoom into a Block                     # Increment Zoom (current)
  desc: |
    Clicking a Block's bullet "zooms" the article surface to show only
    that Block and its descendants. The zoomed Block appears at the top
    as a large bold header; its child tree renders below. A small
    "← Outline" link returns to the unzoomed (full outline) view.

    Zoom state lives in BlockArticle's local state and is propagated to
    BlockNode via ZoomContext so any descendant bullet can be the next
    zoom target. Backlink counts continue to reflect the FULL outline
    tree's incoming refs (not just the zoomed subtree) so badges remain
    accurate at every zoom level.

    Reference-only Blocks are not zoomable — clicking their bullet is a
    no-op (their target's bullet should be clicked instead).
  req F-Zoom.in:
    given: there is a Block X with one or more children
    when: I click X's Block Bullet
    then:
      - X is rendered as a header at the top of the article surface in
        large bold font
      - X's children render below the header recursively
      - the unzoomed outline view is no longer shown
      - a "← Outline" link is visible to return to the unzoomed view
  req F-Zoom.out:
    given: the article surface is currently zoomed into a Block
    when: I click the "← Outline" link
    then: the article returns to the unzoomed view, showing the full
      outline tree from the root.
  req F-Zoom.reference-only:
    given: a reference-only Block (F-V6)
    when: I click its bullet
    then: nothing happens (zooming is disabled for reference-only Blocks).
```

```mdl
feat F-Nav: Arrow-key navigation between bullets   # Increment Nav (current)
  desc: |
    The unmodified ArrowUp / ArrowDown keys move the cursor to the
    beginning of the previous / next *visible* bullet. "Visible" means
    the Block would be rendered given the current expand/collapse state
    of all ancestors — independent of scroll position. If the target is
    outside the viewport, the page scrolls just enough to reveal it.
    Single-line bullets don't need within-paragraph line navigation, so
    arrow keys always cross to the adjacent bullet.

    Implementation note: the navigation walks the rendered DOM
    (`[data-block-id]` elements in document order) rather than reaching
    into the ECHO tree. Because collapsed children aren't mounted, DOM
    order naturally matches the visible-tree order. Both arrow commands
    always return true to ProseMirror's keymap so the browser never
    scrolls the page as a side-effect of an edge-case no-op.

  req F-Nav.up:
    given: cursor is in Block X, and Block Y is the bullet immediately
      above X in the rendered tree (visible, regardless of scroll position)
    when: user presses ArrowUp
    then: caret moves to the start of Y; the page scrolls to bring Y
      into view if it was off-screen.
  req F-Nav.up.edge:
    given: cursor is in Block X, and there is no visible Block above X
    when: user presses ArrowUp
    then: caret does not move; the page does not scroll.
  req F-Nav.down:
    given: cursor is in Block X, and Block Y is the bullet immediately
      below X in the rendered tree (visible, regardless of scroll position)
    when: user presses ArrowDown
    then: caret moves to the start of Y; the page scrolls to bring Y
      into view if it was off-screen.
  req F-Nav.down.edge:
    given: cursor is in Block X, and there is no visible Block below X
    when: user presses ArrowDown
    then: caret does not move; the page does not scroll.
```

```mdl
feat F-V6: Reference-only Block               # Increment V6 (current)
  desc: |
    A Block whose content is exactly one inline ref segment (and no
    meaningful text) is a "reference-only" Block. It is rendered as a
    stand-in for its target:
    - The Block's editor content shows the target's current label live
      (already produced by the inline ref's NodeView).
    - The Block Bullet gains an additional dashed outer ring on top of
      the existing dark dot + shaded halo, signalling that this row is a
      pure reference rather than original content.
    - The backlink count badge on this Block reflects the TARGET's
      backlink count, not the reference-only Block's own — visually this
      Block IS the target.
    - The bullet is non-interactive (reference-only Blocks are leaves;
      they have no children to toggle).
  req F-V6.1:
    when: a Block's content has exactly one segment of kind 'ref' AND no text segment with non-whitespace characters
    then: the Block is treated as reference-only.
  req F-V6.2:
    when: a Block is reference-only
    then:
      - the Block Bullet renders the dark dot, the shaded halo, AND a dashed outer ring around the halo
      - the bullet is non-interactive (no toggle on click)
      - the backlink count rendered to the right of the row is db.query.referencedBy(target) — i.e. the count for the ref's target Block, not this Block.
  req F-V6.3:
    when: a Block is not reference-only
    then: it follows the regular Block Bullet rules from F-V2.
```

```mdl
feat F-V3: Inline ref styling                 # Increment V3 (current)
  desc: |
    Inline ref nodes render with link-style typography — distinct color
    (link blue), no underline by default — so users can scan a paragraph
    and see at a glance which spans are graph references vs. plain text.
  req F-V3.1: the rendered span for a ref node carries a class that applies link-blue color and no underline.
  req F-V3.2: hovering the ref shows a subtle affordance (cursor change or underline-on-hover) without changing layout.
```

```mdl
feat F-V4: Inline backlink count badge        # Increment V4 (current)
  desc: |
    Each Block that is referenced by other Blocks displays a small inline
    count badge to the right of its row. Counts are produced by the
    useBacklinks hook and propagated via React context to BlockNode.

    Live-update guarantee: the count must refresh as soon as the ref node
    renders in the source Block — not after the user navigates away or
    presses Enter. Implementation note: a `db.query(...).subscribe` only
    fires on result-SET changes, not on field-level mutations to existing
    Blocks. The hook subscribes to each Block via `Obj.subscribe` and
    re-binds those subs whenever the Block set changes. This ensures
    that `Obj.update(sourceBlock, m => m.content = [...with ref])`
    triggers a recompute synchronously, and the badge re-renders on the
    next paint frame.
  req F-V4.live:
    when: source Block X is mutated to include an inline ref to target Block Y (via @-mention picker, paste, programmatic update, or any other path)
    then:
      - within one paint frame after X's content is committed, Y's count badge increments by one
      - the user is NOT required to leave X, press Enter, navigate away, or reload
  req F-V4.placement:
    when: a Block has at least one incoming ref
    then: the count badge is rendered immediately to the right of the Block's text content (not pushed to the row's far edge); when text wraps to multiple lines, the badge sits at the end of the last line on which the text fits, rather than at a fixed right margin.
  desc: |
    Each Block that is referenced by other Blocks displays a small inline
    count badge after its content showing how many bullets (or other ECHO
    objects) point at it. Powered by db.query.referencedBy(); the badge
    updates live as refs are added or removed. Clicking the badge opens
    the linked-references panel from F-5 (when that lands).
  req F-V4.1:
    when: at least one inline ref points at a Block
    then: a small "[N]" badge renders inline after the bullet's text content.
  req F-V4.2: the badge count reflects live state (re-runs on referencedBy changes).
  req F-V4.3:
    when: no inline refs point at a Block
    then: no badge is rendered.
  note: |
    Visual reference: in Tana, the badge appears as a small bordered "1"
    next to the parent block. The same affordance reveals the backlinks
    list on click.
```

```mdl
feat F-4b: Live rename propagation   # Increment 4b (current)
  desc: |
    The RefNodeView subscribes to the resolved target via Obj.subscribe at
    mount and re-binds whenever its `dxn` attribute changes. Any field
    mutation on the target — including a rename — fires the subscription
    callback and refreshes the rendered label, with no local edit required.
  req F-4b.1:
    when: a target object's label changes (Obj.update m.name = ...) anywhere
    then: every visible inline ref pointing at that target re-renders with the new label
  req F-4b.2:
    when: an inline ref's dxn attribute changes (e.g., re-targeting in a future increment)
    then: the previous target's subscription is released and the new target's is bound
  req F-4b.3:
    when: the editor unmounts (block re-parented, outline closed)
    then: all ref subscriptions are released via NodeView.destroy
```

```mdl
feat F-5: Backlinks panel       # Increment 5 (current)
  desc: |
    A "Linked references" panel renders below the BlockTree in every Block
    Outline. It lists Blocks elsewhere in the database whose inline ref
    segments point at any Block in this outline's tree (or the outline
    itself). Self-references (a bullet inside the outline mentioning
    another bullet inside the same outline) are excluded — those are
    visible in the tree itself.
    Implementation note: ECHO's referencedBy() introspects typed Ref
    fields, but our refs live inside StructuredContent union segments
    which referencedBy doesn't traverse, so the panel performs a manual
    scan across all Blocks. A typed `Block.references` sidecar can replace
    the scan in a perf increment.
  req F-5.1: a Linked-references panel renders below the BlockTree on any BlockOutline article surface.
  req F-5.2:
    when: at least one Block elsewhere has a content RefSpan whose target is in this outline's tree
    then: that source Block is listed with its label and the target's label.
  req F-5.3:
    when: no incoming refs exist
    then: the panel renders nothing (no empty header).
  req F-5.4: panel updates within one tick of the outline being mutated locally.
```

```mdl
feat F-6: Tags and fields       # Increment 6
  req F-6.1: A Block can be tagged via picker; TagDef is created on demand.
  req F-6.2: Tagged Block renders typed fields inline below the bullet.
```

```mdl
feat F-7: Tana Paste import     # Increment 7
  req F-7.1: Pasting a payload starting with %%tana%% into a BlockOutline imports the structure as Blocks.
  req F-7.2: Supertags, fields, references, and dates are preserved using a stable-ID translation table.
```

## Acceptance

```mdl
test T-1: Increment 1 — scaffold renders
  given: composer-app is running with isLabs enabled
  when: user creates a Block Outline from the navigator
  then:
    - BlockOutline + empty root Block appear in ECHO
    - BlockArticle surface renders the placeholder
    - no console errors
```

```mdl
test T-2: Increment 2 — editable bullet round-trips
  given: composer-app is running, a Block Outline has been created
  when: user types "hello world" into the editor and reloads the page
  then:
    - Block.content === [{ kind: 'text', text: 'hello world' }]
    - on reload, the editor mounts with "hello world" visible
    - no console errors attributable to plugin-blocks
```

```mdl
test T-3: Increment 3 — Enter creates a new sibling
  given: a Block Outline with a single bullet "first"
  when: user places cursor at end of "first" and presses Enter, then types "second"
  then:
    - rootBlock.children has two Block refs in order
    - first Block.content === [{ kind: 'text', text: 'first' }]
    - second Block.content === [{ kind: 'text', text: 'second' }]
    - on reload, both bullets render with original text
```

```mdl
test T-4: Increment 3 — DXN copy
  given: a Block Outline with a bullet, navigator.clipboard available
  when: user right-clicks the bullet
  then: Obj.getDXN(block) is written to the clipboard
```

```mdl
test T-5: Increment 3b — Tab indents
  given: an outline with two top-level bullets [A, B]
  when: cursor is in B and user presses Tab
  then:
    - rootBlock.children === [A] (B removed from top level)
    - A.children === [B] (B is now A's child)
    - B.children unchanged (B keeps its own subtree)
    - B's editor has focus after re-mount
```

```mdl
test T-6: Increment 3b — Shift+Tab dedents
  given: an outline where A.children === [B]
  when: cursor is in B and user presses Shift+Tab
  then:
    - A.children === [] (B removed from A)
    - rootBlock.children === [A, B] (B is now sibling of A)
    - B's editor has focus after re-mount
```

```mdl
test T-7: Increment 4 — @-mention inserts a ref
  given: an outline with at least one other ECHO object available in the space
  when: user types "hello @" then types a query that matches an object's label, then clicks the object
  then:
    - the @-and-query range is replaced by an inline ref node
    - Block.content contains a RefSpan segment with target pointing at the chosen object
    - the rendered text shows the target's current label
    - on reload the ref persists with the same DXN
```

```mdl
test T-9: Increment V2 — collapse / expand parent
  given: an outline where bullet A has one child bullet B
  when: user clicks A's bullet marker
  then:
    - A's bullet renders in the collapsed (hollow) style with a "1" badge
    - B is no longer visible in the tree
    - A.state.expanded === false in ECHO
  when: user clicks A's bullet marker again
  then:
    - A's bullet renders in the open (filled) style
    - B is visible again
    - A.state.expanded === true (or the field is reset)
```

```mdl
test T-V4-live: Adding a ref increments the target's count without leaving the source
  given: an outline with a Block X (currently being edited) and another Block Y; Y currently has zero or N references
  when: user inserts an inline ref to Y inside X — e.g. types @ in X, picks Y from the picker
  then:
    - within one paint frame after the ref node renders inside X, Y's count badge displays previous_count + 1
    - the user does not need to press Enter, leave X, navigate away, or reload for the badge to appear
```

```mdl
test T-10: Increment 5 — backlinks panel lists external refs
  given: outline X has bullet B; outline Y has a bullet whose content @-mentions B
  when: user opens outline X
  then: the "Linked references" panel below X's tree lists the source bullet from Y, with label and target label
  when: outline X has no incoming refs
  then: no panel renders
```

```mdl
test T-8: Increment 4b — rename propagates without local edit
  given: an outline that contains an inline ref to a target object T with label "alpha"
  when: T is renamed to "beta" by another panel or peer (no local edit in the outline)
  then:
    - within one tick, the rendered label in the outline updates from "alpha" to "beta"
    - no manual reload or focus-change is required
```

```mdl
test T-Cmd-Shift-Enter: Cmd+Shift+Enter inserts an empty sibling ABOVE
  given: an outline with a single bullet X containing some text
  when: cursor is in X and user presses Cmd+Shift+Enter (Mod+Shift+Enter)
  then:
    - parent.children has two Block refs in order [Y, X]
    - Y.content is empty
    - X.content is unchanged
    - cursor lands at the start of Y (Y has focus)
```

